      "content": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "c20u2",
            "text": "A controlled follow-up to our Qwen3.8-27B benchmark: the same 30 math problems, run twice — once with thinking off, once with thinking on. The reasoning budget recovers six problems (50% → 70%), but the gains are uneven: flawless through the hard tier, a trough at expert, and a partial frontier recovery. Plus the latency cost of the whole thing.\n",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "3gp2n",
            "text": "The follow-up question",
            "type": "header-two"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [
              {
                "key": 0,
                "length": 40,
                "offset": 8
              }
            ],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 13,
                "offset": 80,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 12,
                "offset": 142,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 2,
                "offset": 232,
                "style": "Italic"
              }
            ],
            "key": "arqfd",
            "text": "When we benchmarked Qwen3.8-27B on the DGX Spark, the one weak number was math: 15/30 (50.0%) in the Official A calibration. But that run was thinking off — the Official A ranking default. The model's enable_thinkingtoggle defaults on, and math is exactly where chain-of-thought reasoning should buy the most.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "8ddp1",
            "text": "So we ran the exact same 30 math problems again, this time with thinking on. Here's the controlled comparison.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "5r73q",
            "text": "The setup",
            "type": "header-two"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 15,
                "offset": 254,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "nm38",
            "text": "VARIABLEVALUEModelQwen/Qwen3.8-27B-FP8 (unchanged)Endpointhttp://spark-56bc:30000/v1 (unchanged)EngineSGLang, EAGLE 3/1/4 spec decode (unchanged)SuiteThe same 30 math tests (v3.math.*, easy → frontier)EvaluatorThe same regex_match against \\boxed{}answersOnly differencechat_template_kwargs.enable_thinking = false vs true",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "a8bsi",
            "text": "This is a clean A/B: identical model, identical hardware, identical prompts, identical scoring. The single changed variable is whether the model reasons before answering.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "cjms",
            "text": "Results",
            "type": "header-two"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "4hd7e",
            "text": "Overall",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 3,
                "offset": 28,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 5,
                "offset": 38,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 2,
                "offset": 53,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 5,
                "offset": 62,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 38,
                "offset": 68,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "afd14",
            "text": "CONFIGURATIONRESULTThinking off15/30 (50.0%)Thinking on21/30 (70.0%)Delta+6 tests, +20.0 percentage points",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "2aqkf",
            "text": "By difficulty",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "4gjrk",
            "text": "TIERTHINKING OFFTHINKING ONDELTAEasy2/2 (100%)2/2 (100%)—Medium2/3 (66.7%)3/3 (100%)+1Hard4/5 (80.0%)5/5 (100%)+1Expert2/8 (25.0%)3/8 (37.5%)+1Frontier5/12 (41.7%)8/12 (66.7%)+3",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "ruiv",
            "text": "What the numbers say",
            "type": "header-two"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 46,
                "offset": 0,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 14,
                "offset": 78,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "6cmm5",
            "text": "Thinking-on cleans up everything below expert. Easy, medium, and hard are now 10/10 flawless. The two gaps in the thinking-off run (one medium, one hard) both closed. If you need reliable arithmetic, algebra, and multi-step computation, thinking on is unambiguous — it turns the bottom three tiers into a perfect sweep.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 37,
                "offset": 0,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 4,
                "offset": 201,
                "style": "Italic"
              },
              {
                "length": 22,
                "offset": 277,
                "style": "Italic"
              }
            ],
            "key": "1m1s0",
            "text": "Expert is the trough — in both modes. 2/8 off, 3/8 on. That's barely a recovery. This is the most interesting finding: the expert-tier problems (7.683, 135.96, −0.01384, −9.417, 29.924 — all failed in both runs) are not being saved by reasoning. The model reasons its way to a confident wrong answer. That's a genuine capability ceiling for this 27B dense architecture on that class of problem, not a thinking-mode artifact.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 64,
                "offset": 0,
                "style": "Bold"
              },
              {
                "length": 23,
                "offset": 169,
                "style": "Italic"
              },
              {
                "length": 3,
                "offset": 278,
                "style": "Italic"
              }
            ],
            "key": "28ho4",
            "text": "Frontier recovers the most — and it's the counterintuitive part. The hardest tier went 5/12 → 8/12 (+3), the biggest absolute gain. That means the frontier problems are solvable with reasoning — they're hard-but-tractable — while the expert tier contains problems that are hard and beyond the model's reach regardless of mode. Difficulty labels don't always track \"what reasoning can recover.\"",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 45,
                "offset": 0,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "27ktj",
            "text": "All failures are wrong answers, not timeouts. Every fail across both runs was a regex did not match — the model produced a number, just the wrong one. Zero timeouts, zero errors, zero format failures. This is a clean capability measurement, not an infrastructure artifact.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "de3h2",
            "text": "The cost: 79 minutes of wall time",
            "type": "header-two"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 26,
                "offset": 46,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "4euhr",
            "text": "Thinking-on is not free. The 30-test run took 4,738 seconds (79 minutes) — an average of ~158 seconds per problem. The thinking-off math portion of the full calibration ran far faster because the model wasn't generating a reasoning chain before each answer.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 20,
                "offset": 93,
                "style": "Italic"
              }
            ],
            "key": "6c8s",
            "text": "That's the real trade-off, and it's why Official A ranks thinking-off: the standard measures capability per token, and reasoning tokens are expensive. But for a math-heavy workload where you need the answer right and latency is acceptable, thinking on is the correct deployment choice.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "2dp0s",
            "text": "CONFIGURATIONMATHWALL TIME (30 TESTS)Thinking off50.0%fast (part of full 157-test run)Thinking on70.0%~79 min standalone",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "73lit",
            "text": "Recommendation",
            "type": "header-two"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 20,
                "offset": 0,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "ur61",
            "text": "Default thinking off for agent/coding/prose workloads — those categories were already strong without reasoning (tool-calling 100%, coding 86.7%).",
            "type": "ordered-list-item"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 25,
                "offset": 0,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "en942",
            "text": "Turn thinking on for math — it's a 20-point swing, and math is where the reasoning budget pays off.",
            "type": "ordered-list-item"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 46,
                "offset": 0,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "321vu",
            "text": "Don't expect thinking to fix expert-tier math. The expert trough is a capability ceiling. If you need that class of problem solved reliably, a larger or math-specialized model is the honest answer.",
            "type": "ordered-list-item"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [
              {
                "length": 23,
                "offset": 0,
                "style": "Bold"
              }
            ],
            "key": "2ho0t",
            "text": "Budget for the latency. Thinking-on math is ~2.6× slower per problem. That's fine for batch/offline work, painful for interactive use.",
            "type": "ordered-list-item"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "2ajuo",
            "text": "Reproducing this",
            "type": "header-two"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [
              {
                "key": 1,
                "length": 19,
                "offset": 55
              }
            ],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "131n1",
            "text": "The comparison script and both result files are in the Nemo Knowledge Base:",
            "type": "unstyled"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "apoqb",
            "text": "scripts/qwen38-math-thinking-on.py — the thinking-on math re-run",
            "type": "unordered-list-item"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "f7thl",
            "text": "results/stage1_cal-qwen38-27b-fp8-strict-v01_*.json — thinking-off (math 15/30)",
            "type": "unordered-list-item"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "5ibd",
            "text": "results/stage1_cal-qwen38-27b-fp8-math-thinking-on_*.json — thinking-on (math 21/30)",
            "type": "unordered-list-item"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "8dn5e",
            "text": "Verification notes",
            "type": "header-two"
          },
          {
            "data": {},
            "entityRanges": [
              {
                "key": 2,
                "length": 30,
                "offset": 389
              }
            ],
            "inlineStyleRanges": [],
            "key": "eok7g",
            "text": "Every number in this post is measured from the live endpoint on 2026-08-17. The two runs differ only in enable_thinking; model, hardware, prompts, and the regex_match evaluator are identical. The thinking-off math results are drawn from the original Official A calibration (2026-08-16), and the thinking-on results from the dedicated re-run (2026-08-17). This post is the follow-up to the original Qwen3.8-27B benchmark.",
            "type": "unstyled"
          }
        ],
